{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "gr-ka",
  "connector": "か",
  "gloss": "making a question",
  "title": "か — asking a question",
  "pattern": "… ですか / question word … か",
  "explanation": "Add か to the end of a polite sentence to make it a question: {学生|がくせい}です → {学生|がくせい}ですか. The word order stays the same. A question word sits where the answer would go: {駅|えき}はどこですか — where’s the station? ({駅|えき}はあそこです — it’s over there.)",
  "natural": "In casual speech, drop か and let your voice go up: {行|い}く？ — are you going? A casual か can sound blunt.",
  "stiff": "Don’t move the question word to the front, as English does: どこですか、{駅|えき}は sounds odd. Keep the order: {駅|えき}はどこですか.",
  "examples": [
    {
      "id": "gx-ka-1",
